---

<Base
  title="Research Program · Xiaochuan Li"
  description="How people and artificial systems understand the concepts, evidence, and norms in language: competing accounts written as models, tested on human behaviour and inside language models."
>
  <article class="rp">
    <h1 class="rp-h1">Research Program</h1>

    <section class="rp-sec">
      <p class="rp-lead">
        I study how people and artificial systems understand the concepts, evidence, and norms in language, and turn them into inferences and choices. Several accounts of how a word is understood
        will usually fit the same behaviour. Finding where they come apart, and stating that
        difference precisely enough to measure, is most of the work. People can be measured from the outside.
        A language model can be opened, so the same account can also be checked against what the
        network represents.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="rp-sec">
      <h2 class="rp-h2"><span class="rp-n">1</span>The problem</h2>
      <p class="rp-p">
        Understanding a word is a computation: something has to get from the evidence a speaker
        has, or the context a hearer is in, to one interpretation rather than another. Theories of
        meaning say what the end states are and which inferences follow from them. They rarely say
        how the end state is arrived at, and they are usually compatible with more than one way of
        arriving at it.
      </p>
      <p class="rp-p">
        Behavioural data does not settle this on its own. A single pattern of judgements or choices
        is normally consistent with several accounts. They were never asked a question on which they
        differ, so collecting more of the same data will not help. Formal semantics states the
        competing analyses precisely, then stops before the algorithm. On the experimental side the
        usual design varies one factor at a time, which answers whether that factor matters and
        leaves the accounts standing. Work on language models reports how closely a model matches
        people, which says nothing about what either of them is computing.
      </p>
      <p class="rp-p">
        What I work on sits between those: taking competing analyses of a phenomenon, writing them
        as models that predict incompatible things, and finding or designing the conditions under
        which the data can choose. An account that cannot be told apart from its rivals will not be
        rescued by more experiments of the same kind. And a model that reproduces a human pattern
        says nothing about meaning until someone has stated what it would be reproducing.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="rp-sec">
      <h2 class="rp-h2"><span class="rp-n">2</span>A worked case: what <em>must</em> requires</h2>
      <p class="rp-p">
        Seeing rain through the window, you say <em>it&rsquo;s raining</em>. Seeing someone come in
        with wet hair, you say <em>it must be raining</em>. The odd part is the first case: with the
        best possible evidence, <em>must</em> gets worse, not better. Something about direct evidence
        rules it out.
      </p>
      <p class="rp-p">
        There is more than one explanation, and they disagree about what <em>must</em> means.
        It may be written into the meaning of <em>must</em> that the evidence has to be indirect.
        It may be that <em>must</em> carries no such condition and the bare assertion simply wins
        the competition whenever the evidence is direct. It may be that the source does nothing
        at all, and what looks like its effect is unmatched evidence strength.
      </p>

      <figure class="rp-fig">
        <svg viewBox="0 0 720 196" role="img" aria-label="Three accounts of the choice between must, might and the bare assertion. In the first, only evidence strength feeds the choice. In the second, strength feeds the choice and the source acts on must directly. In the third, the source acts on the bare assertion, which competes with must.">
          <g class="fg">
            <text class="cap" x="6" y="16">(a) strength only</text>
            <rect x="6" y="36" width="104" height="34" rx="2"/>
            <text class="lb" x="58" y="58" text-anchor="middle">strength</text>
            <path d="M58 70 L58 112" marker-end="url(#rp-ar)"/>
            <rect x="6" y="114" width="104" height="34" rx="2"/>
            <text class="lb" x="58" y="136" text-anchor="middle">choice</text>

            <text class="cap" x="246" y="16">(b) source in the meaning</text>
            <rect x="246" y="36" width="104" height="34" rx="2"/>
            <text class="lb" x="298" y="58" text-anchor="middle">strength</text>
            <rect x="370" y="36" width="104" height="34" rx="2"/>
            <text class="lb" x="422" y="58" text-anchor="middle">source</text>
            <path d="M298 70 L298 112" marker-end="url(#rp-ar)"/>
            <path d="M422 70 L422 95 L350 95 L350 112" marker-end="url(#rp-ar)"/>
            <rect x="246" y="114" width="104" height="34" rx="2"/>
            <text class="lb" x="298" y="136" text-anchor="middle">choice</text>
            <text class="sm" x="360" y="108">on <tspan font-style="italic">must</tspan></text>

            <text class="cap" x="520" y="16">(c) source via competition</text>
            <rect x="520" y="36" width="90" height="34" rx="2"/>
            <text class="lb" x="565" y="58" text-anchor="middle">source</text>
            <path d="M565 70 L565 92" marker-end="url(#rp-ar)"/>
            <rect x="520" y="94" width="90" height="30" rx="2"/>
            <text class="lb" x="565" y="114" text-anchor="middle">bare</text>
            <path d="M610 109 L666 109" marker-end="url(#rp-bar)"/>
            <rect x="630" y="36" width="84" height="34" rx="2"/>
            <text class="lb" x="672" y="58" text-anchor="middle">must</text>
            <path d="M672 70 L672 96" marker-end="url(#rp-ar)"/>
            <text class="sm" x="620" y="146">competes</text>
          </g>
          <defs>
            <marker id="rp-ar" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="8" refY="5" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto-start-reverse">
              <path d="M0 1 L9 5 L0 9" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.6"/>
            </marker>
            <marker id="rp-bar" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="7" refY="5" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto">
              <path d="M6 1 L6 9" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8"/>
            </marker>
          </defs>
        </svg>
        <figcaption><b>Figure 1.</b> Where the three accounts disagree. Holding evidence strength fixed, (b) predicts a direct effect of source on <em>must</em>; (c) predicts the same surface pattern with no such term, because the bare assertion wins when the evidence is direct; (a) predicts the effect disappears once strength is matched.</figcaption>
      </figure>
      <p class="rp-p">
        All three fit the published production data. They are told apart by where they disagree:
        with strength held fixed, does the source still act on <em>must</em> directly, or only
        through the competition with the bare assertion? Written as probabilistic choice models
        over the available expressions, that question becomes a model comparison, and the comparison
        can be checked for whether this design can separate the three accounts at all.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="rp-sec">
      <h2 class="rp-h2"><span class="rp-n">3</span>How a comparison is run</h2>
      <p class="rp-p">
        Each account becomes a probabilistic choice model: given the properties of a situation, it
        returns a distribution over the expressions a speaker could use. The accounts differ in
        which properties enter and how, and they differ in how many free parameters that takes,
        so a comparison has to charge for complexity rather than reward the most flexible model.
        Fitting is to trial-level data; selection is by the usual criteria and by held-out
        prediction.
      </p>
      <p class="rp-p">
        Before any of that is worth reporting, the design has to be able to separate the accounts at
        all. Generate data from each model and see whether the fitting procedure recovers the one
        that produced it. If they can imitate each other here, no amount of real data from that
        design will choose between them, and what has been learned is about the materials rather
        than the phenomenon. Whatever survives then has to predict conditions it was not fitted to:
        items held out, or a second experiment on the same phenomenon with a different task.
      </p>
      <p class="rp-p">
        A comparison that separates nothing is still an outcome, and it arrives with a specification
        of what would separate them: which factors to cross, which to match, which measurement
        error in the original materials to remove. That specification is a reason to run a new
        study, which is more than most studies begin with.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="rp-sec">
      <h2 class="rp-h2"><span class="rp-n">4</span>Two kinds of language user</h2>
      <p class="rp-p">
        The same account can be fitted to the choices a language model makes over the same
        expressions. This is not a test of whether the model resembles a person. It asks whether
        one account covers a second system with an entirely different history, and where it fails
        to.
      </p>

      <figure class="rp-fig">
        <svg viewBox="0 0 720 174" role="img" aria-label="Two kinds of language user. For people, an account can be tested against behaviour only; what is inside is not reachable. For a language model, the same account can be tested against behaviour and, in addition, against its representations by intervening on them.">
          <g class="fg">
            <text class="cap" x="6" y="15">people</text>
            <rect x="6" y="32" width="112" height="34" rx="2"/>
            <text class="lb" x="62" y="54" text-anchor="middle">an account</text>
            <path d="M118 49 L186 49" marker-end="url(#rp-ar2)"/>
            <rect x="188" y="32" width="112" height="34" rx="2"/>
            <text class="lb" x="244" y="54" text-anchor="middle">behaviour</text>
            <g class="dash">
              <path d="M118 90 L186 90"/>
              <rect x="188" y="73" width="112" height="34" rx="2"/>
            </g>
            <text class="lb dim" x="244" y="95" text-anchor="middle">what is inside</text>
            <text class="sm" x="6" y="95">stops here</text>

            <text class="cap" x="400" y="15">language models</text>
            <rect x="400" y="32" width="112" height="34" rx="2"/>
            <text class="lb" x="456" y="54" text-anchor="middle">an account</text>
            <path d="M512 49 L580 49" marker-end="url(#rp-ar2)"/>
            <rect x="582" y="32" width="112" height="34" rx="2"/>
            <text class="lb" x="638" y="54" text-anchor="middle">behaviour</text>
            <path d="M512 90 L580 90" marker-end="url(#rp-ar2)"/>
            <rect x="582" y="73" width="112" height="34" rx="2"/>
            <text class="lb" x="638" y="95" text-anchor="middle">representations</text>
            <text class="sm" x="400" y="95">and here</text>
            <path d="M638 107 L638 130" marker-end="url(#rp-ar2)"/>
            <text class="sm" x="638" y="146" text-anchor="middle">intervene</text>
          </g>
          <defs>
            <marker id="rp-ar2" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="8" refY="5" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto-start-reverse">
              <path d="M0 1 L9 5 L0 9" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.6"/>
            </marker>
          </defs>
        </svg>
        <figcaption><b>Figure 2.</b> Why both kinds of user are needed. The same account is tested against behaviour on either side. Only in a model can the variables it posits be looked for directly, by intervening on them and checking that the predicted responses move while the others hold still.</figcaption>
      </figure>
      <p class="rp-p">
        The asymmetry is the reason for working on both. With people, measurement stops at
        behaviour. In a model, the variables an account posits can be looked for directly: if
        strength and source are represented separately, intervening on one should move
        <em>must</em> and leave <em>might</em> alone. An account that survives behaviour but fails
        that test was describing the output, not the computation.
        <a href="https://jmlr.org/papers/v26/23-0058.html">Causal abstraction</a> and
        <a href="https://proceedings.mlr.press/v236/geiger24a.html">distributed alignment search</a>
        give this a usable definition. Alignment in a model is evidence about the model. It does
        not show that people compute the same way.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="rp-sec">
      <h2 class="rp-h2"><span class="rp-n">5</span>What the input has to contain</h2>
      <p class="rp-p">
        Behaviour tells you what a mature system does, not what it needed in order to get there.
        That question has a second dimension: a user whose input is limited. Children are one such
        case, and a model trained on a developmentally plausible corpus is another, with the
        difference that its input can be edited.
      </p>
      <p class="rp-p">
        This is where the accounts make their sharpest predictions. If the requirement is part of
        the meaning, the evidence for it has to be in the input somewhere; if it comes from
        competition with the bare assertion, removing the contexts that set up that competition
        should change the pattern. Whether such evidence is dense enough in what children actually
        hear is an empirical question about corpora, answerable before any model is trained.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="rp-sec">
      <h2 class="rp-h2"><span class="rp-n">6</span>Where this goes</h2>
      <p class="rp-p">
        What follows assumes a comparison that has already separated its candidates. Without that,
        none of it is answerable.
      </p>
      <p class="rp-p">
        An account that specifies which variables are involved also specifies what a learner has to
        acquire, and in what order. Children are the case where
        the end state is still being assembled, so a developmental version of the same contrast
        can show whether the components come apart in time.
      </p>
      <p class="rp-p">
        Where an account posits distinct variables, a model can be asked whether it keeps them
        distinct. Intervene on one, and the responses the account predicts should move while the
        rest hold still. Behaviour reaches this in neither kind of user, and it is what separates
        an account of the output from an account of the computation.
      </p>
      <p class="rp-p">
        With the input under control the requirements become removable. Take out the evidence an
        account says is necessary and the capacity should fail to form; take out something else and
        it should not. That is the strongest form of the question, and it leads back to what
        children actually hear.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="rp-sec">
      <h2 class="rp-h2"><span class="rp-n">7</span>Where the projects sit</h2>
      <figure class="rp-fig rp-fig-tab">
      <table class="rp-tab">
        <thead><tr><th></th><th>Limited input</th><th>Mature</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">People</th>
            <td><a href="/research/learnability">preschool children</a></td>
            <td><a href="/research/modal-language-models">model comparison on human data</a></td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">Language models</th>
            <td>controlled input, not started</td>
            <td>behaviour, then representations</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <figcaption><b>Table 1.</b> The two dimensions: whose language use, and whether the input that produced it can be controlled. Only the lower left allows the question of what the input had to contain to be answered by intervention.</figcaption>
      </figure>
      <p class="rp-p">
        <a href="/research/typicality-in-referent-choice">The typicality paper</a> and
        <a href="/research/three-ways">Three Ways of Leaving <em>p</em> Unsettled</a> do the step
        that comes before all of this: finding where a contrast lies and stating the competing
        explanations, which is what a model can then be written for.
        <a href="/research/latent-control-states">The moral-framing project</a> is on a
        different question, how a moral sentence changes a model’s dilemma choices and what happens
        inside the model when it does, and is where the intervention methods are being learned.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="rp-sec">
      <h2 class="rp-h2"><span class="rp-n">8</span>What this does not claim</h2>
      <p class="rp-p">
        No account has won yet; the comparison has not been run. A model comparison that cannot
        separate its candidates is a result about the design, and saying so is part of the work.
        The projects constrain one another without sharing a dataset or a deadline, and a mismatch
        between them can come from a weak analysis, an inadequate model, a measurement problem, or
        a genuinely different computation. Telling those apart is the work. A shared mechanism is not assumed in advance.
      </p>
    </section>

    <p class="rp-foot"><a href="/research">See how this program is being developed in the current work →</a></p>
  </article>
</Base>

